 their effectiveness or increasing side effects. Although specific mechanisms are not detailed in the provided evidence, the acknowledgment of dietary impact highlights the need for patient education on avoiding certain foods during treatment. Given the narrow therapeutic index of many psychiatric medications, even modest changes in bioavailability due to food can have clinically significant consequences [Data: Entities (4173); Relationships (32156)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Erythromycin stearate absorption is reduced by food\",\n            \"explanation\": \"硬酯酸红霉素 (erythromycin stearate) exhibits decreased absorption when taken with food, supporting recommendations for administration one hour before or two hours after meals. This is consistent with the broader class behavior of macrolide antibiotics, where food can delay gastric emptying or alter dissolution kinetics, leading to subtherapeutic concentrations. Proper timing relative to meals is essential to ensure adequate antimicrobial activity [Data: Entities (7477); Relationships (46769)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Compound ranitidine interacts with antacids, not directly with food\",\n            \"explanation\": \"复方雷尼替丁 (compound ranitidine) is linked not to food but to 抗酸药 (antacids) in the provided data, with co-administration shown to reduce serum concentrations of both ranitidine and bismuth citrate. This suggests that while food interactions may exist, the primary documented concern is with concomitant use of acid-neutralizing agents. This distinction is important for clinical counseling—patients should be advised about antacid timing rather than general dietary restrictions unless further evidence emerges [Data: Entities (4787); Relationships (34953)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Dietary guidance varies significantly across drug classes\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The community illustrates that food-drug interaction